WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.335
Lesson 6.7.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:07.100
Valuing DeFi protocols: fees, revenue, earnings and multiples.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.450
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.919
to compare protocols on their economics, not their narratives.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:15.509
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:17.194
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.194 --> 00:00:19.909
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.909 --> 00:00:21.434
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.434 --> 00:00:23.596
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:27.421
First, Fees: everything users pay.

00:00:27.421 --> 00:00:30.714
Revenue: the part that goes to the protocol

00:00:30.714 --> 00:00:34.606
or token holders (the rest goes to LPs/lenders).

00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:38.063
Earnings: revenue minus token incentives paid out.

00:00:38.063 --> 00:00:42.212
Next, Multiples: price-to-fees (P/F), price-to-sales

00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:45.878
(P/S, on revenue), price-to-earnings (P/E).

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:47.968
Use FDV as well as market

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:51.243
cap, since unlocks will arrive (6.2).

00:00:51.243 --> 00:00:55.904
Then, Quality checks: is revenue growing without rising incentives?

00:00:55.904 --> 00:00:58.664
Is it concentrated in one pool or chain?

00:00:58.664 --> 00:01:00.669
Does it survive a bear market?

00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:04.024
Does the token actually receive it (value capture)?

00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:06.826
And finally, Multiples compare protocols; they

00:01:06.826 --> 00:01:08.376
don't set a "right" price.

00:01:09.676 --> 00:01:12.191
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:12.191 --> 00:01:16.843
FDV $2B, annual revenue $100M, token

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:21.166
incentives $60M → earnings $40M.

00:01:21.166 --> 00:01:28.041
P/S (FDV) = 20×; P/E (FDV) = 50×.

00:01:28.041 --> 00:01:31.279
A rival with the same revenue but

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:35.088
no incentives has earnings of $100M: at

00:01:35.088 --> 00:01:38.054
the same FDV its P/E is 20×.

00:01:38.054 --> 00:01:40.566
Same revenue, very different economics.

00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:43.326
Here's your checklist.

00:01:43.326 --> 00:01:44.861
Do it now, for real.

00:01:44.861 --> 00:01:48.785
First, Fees, revenue and earnings separated, with sources.

00:01:48.785 --> 00:01:52.200
Next, Multiples on FDV and market cap.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.614
And finally, Revenue quality and value capture assessed.

00:01:56.914 --> 00:01:58.124
Question 1.

00:01:58.124 --> 00:01:59.664
Revenue vs earnings?

00:02:04.084 --> 00:02:06.625
The answer: Earnings are revenue minus

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.905
token incentives (and other costs).

00:02:10.205 --> 00:02:11.450
Question 2.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.645
Why use FDV in multiples?

00:02:18.065 --> 00:02:21.294
The answer: Future unlocks will add supply;

00:02:21.294 --> 00:02:23.789
FDV shows the fully diluted price.

00:02:25.089 --> 00:02:26.359
Question 3.

00:02:26.359 --> 00:02:30.539
FDV $600M, earnings $20M.

00:02:30.539 --> 00:02:31.554
P/E?

00:02:35.974 --> 00:02:37.559
The answer: 30×.

00:02:38.859 --> 00:02:40.104
Let's recap.

00:02:40.104 --> 00:02:42.119
Fees: everything users pay.

00:02:42.119 --> 00:02:49.564
Multiples: price-to-fees (P/F), price-to-sales (P/S, on revenue), price-to-earnings (P/E).

00:02:49.564 --> 00:02:53.696
Quality checks: is revenue growing without rising incentives?

00:02:53.696 --> 00:02:56.456
Is it concentrated in one pool or chain?

00:02:56.456 --> 00:02:58.461
Does it survive a bear market?

00:02:58.461 --> 00:03:01.606
Does the token actually receive it (value capture)?.

00:03:01.606 --> 00:03:05.251
Multiples compare protocols: they don't set a "right" price.

00:03:06.551 --> 00:03:09.066
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:12.371
Next up, Lesson 7.0: Mastery Starter.
